-- I27 (#2188) S2-DECL (d) bullet 3 -- TWO admitted declarations that DISAGREE.
-- Its sibling i28 is this same graph with `impl IA String` removed; that ONE impl is the
-- whole delta between the agreeing cell and this one, and it is what the deleted #1664
-- pin's own claim.txt named as the construction of the disagreeing arm it did not cover.
--
-- Both `IA` and `IZ` are admitted (each named in its own import clause -- S1-NS (a)'s
-- TYPE arm), and they denote DIFFERENT things:
--   * `IA.mth : a -> Int -> Int` dispatches at argument 0, head String, and
--     `impl IA String` EXISTS => IA denotes that impl (55).
--   * `IZ.mth : Int -> b -> Int` dispatches at argument 1, head Int, and no
--     `impl IZ Int` exists anywhere => IZ denotes the imported standalone (99).
-- Two admitted, two denotations => (d) bullet 3: a LOCATED REJECT at the occurrence.
-- Not 55, not 99, not 7: with two admitted candidates and no discriminator written in
-- the source, any value here would be a tie-break "supplied from outside the program the
-- author can write down" (SHADOW-SEMANTICS.md, S2-DECL (d)).
--
-- 🚨 WHY THIS CELL PINS A VERDICT AND NOT A VALUE, and why that is not weaker here: at
-- base 7e5ec5e7 this program COMPILED, and printed 99 with these import clauses in this
-- order and 55 with them swapped -- on run AND on the shipped binary. A value pin could
-- have been satisfied by either half of an order-dependent coin flip. The order-swapped
-- companion is the S2-DECL (e) half; the two rows together say "rejects, and rejects
-- either way round".
--
-- `fmodI.mth` is the load-bearing standalone shadow: without it `mth` is not a shadow and
-- none of the S2-DECL machinery is reached.
import fmodI.{mth}
import amodI.{IA, af}
import zmodI.{IZ, zf}

main = println (mth "abc" 5 + af 0 + zf 0)
